+Do these in order. Each one finds problems the others miss, and most serious problems only show up when a changed line is read
+against code that did not change.
+
+1. Read each changed file whole, then say to yourself what each hunk was for.
+2. Compare. Go through the closest sibling line by line against the new code, and the old version against the new. What does the
+   sibling do that this does not: a cleanup, a delete rule, a validation, a filter, an error path, a registration, a test? What did
+   the old code guarantee that is now gone? Use the repository's own helper or pattern as the standard.
+3. Follow every changed name. For each function, option, setting, environment variable, field or route added or changed, search for
+   all its readers and writers. Is one guarded where another is not? Does every caller still fit? Is the new thing reachable at all?
+4. Test the claims. For each promise in the title, description, docstrings and README, find the code that delivers it, then try it
+   with the value absent, empty, malformed and repeated.
+5. Follow failure. From each throw, rejected promise, error return or default value, trace what the caller or user finally sees. Does
+   a missing setting fail safe? Does a catch report success?
+6. Follow outside data. Did this change alter who can write a value that reaches HTML, SQL, a shell, a path or a URL?
+7. Walk through it with one concrete input: a loop, a generated string, a quoted shell line, a template, a regex. Does each call do
+   anything, or is it a save with no change, a value computed and never used, a request made twice?
+8. Repeat your findings. When you find a defect, look for the same pattern in the other changed files, and report each file as a
+   finding of its own.
